From 78a282ac5de6a8fb093f458b75d45a1451e33a4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 08:37:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(process): persisted jobs survive a restart of any
 spawning user unit [daveey]

Background jobs started from hub chats died on every hub update, even with
persist_on_release=true (a CI supervisor twice on 2026-11-04; ~every 80 min).
Desktop's hub backend runs in hermes-always-on-dashboard.service
(KillMode=control-group), which each update restarts; persisted jobs left
their spawner's cgroup only when it was a Kanban run scope (#804).

- process_registry_persist_scope: a persist job whose spawner runs in any
  systemd user unit gets its own `systemd-run --user --scope`
  (hermes-worker-<proc>.scope, session.systemd_unit set so kill works).
  Kanban runs keep hermes-kanban.slice and the run cap; other spawners use
  app.slice with the spawner unit's own cap (infinity when uncapped). No user
  unit / no user bus: unchanged.
- A persisted job also writes to the process-output log instead of a pipe
  (POSIX): with only the scope, its first write after the spawner exited was
  SIGPIPE (proven by the chatty integration case). The next backend re-adopts
  the log. A persisted pty=true request runs on the log and says why
  (pty_fallback / stdin error), instead of claiming an isolated worker.
- Killed-job receipts (update pause and backend_shutdown) advise
  "restart it with persist_on_release=true so it survives updates"; the
  persist_on_release schema text says it survives restarts and updates.

Tests: unit (fake app.slice/x.service cgroup -> scope argv; capped spawner;
system/login/no unit unchanged), Linux integration (transient user service
spawns a persist job, service stopped, job survives; chatty variant keeps
printing), receipt hint. RED before, GREEN after.

Self-heal: other-8101e835f4, gateway-67e85ef16a, other-ab3e1e2c74.
